 == Changes to SPI
 
 This section describes changes to contracts (classes, interfaces, methods, etc.) which are considered https://hibernate.org/community/compatibility-policy/#spi[SPI].
+
+[[client-enhancement]]
+=== Client enhancement
+
+`Enhancer.enhanceClient(String, byte[])` performs client field-access rewriting separately from managed-class enhancement.
+Its default implementation reports unsupported client enhancement, so existing enhancer implementations continue to work when it is unused.
+`HibernatePersistenceProvider.getClientClassTransformer(PersistenceUnitInfo, Map)` exposes an independent Jakarta transformer for containers.
+Containers must still arrange managed-class enhancement; requesting the client transformer does not register either transformer.
+
+Gradle, Maven, and Ant accept `enableClientEnhancement`, defaulting to false.
+`enableExtendedEnhancement` remains a deprecated alias. When both are set, the new name wins, including explicit false.
+Client classes no longer receive a managed-enhancement marker simply because client enhancement was requested.
+Targets must be scheduled for managed enhancement or already carry compatible enhancement; known skipped or incompatible targets fail during enhancement instead of producing unavailable accessor calls.
+XML mapping recognition is unchanged, and reflection and method-handle accesses are outside the client transformation.
+
+[[enhancement-session-spi]]
+=== Enhancement model, options, and environment
+
+`EnhancementContext`, `DefaultEnhancementContext`, and `EnhancementContextWrapper`
+have been replaced by independent `EnhancementModel`, `EnhancementOptions`, and
+`EnhancementEnvironment` contracts. `DefaultEnhancementModel` supplies the existing
+annotation-based interpretation. Discovery and metadata belong to an
+`EnhancementSession`, not to the model or options.
+
+`BytecodeProvider` implementations must replace `getEnhancer(context)` with
+`createEnhancementSession(model, environment)`. There is no compatibility adapter.
+Direct `EnhancerImpl` construction is replaced by session creation as well:
+
+[source,java]
+----
+try (var session = provider.createEnhancementSession(
+        model, EnhancementEnvironment.forClassLoader(loader))) {
+    var enhancer = session.createEnhancer(
+            EnhancementOptions.of(dirtyTracking, lazyInitialization, associationManagement));
+    session.discoverTypes(className, originalBytes);
+    byte[] enhanced = enhancer.enhance(className, originalBytes);
+}
+----
+
+Environments resolve bytecode without defining application classes and may use a
+custom byte source instead of a loader. The Byte Buddy provider also accepts a
+borrowed `EnhancerClassLocator` when creating a session. Sessions do not close
+borrowed environments, locators, or class loaders.
+
+Call `invalidateMetadata()` between passes which replace class resources. This
+clears bytecode and getter caches while retaining discovered persistence
+classifications. Close or invalidate only when no session operations are active.
+Closing a session makes all of its enhancers unusable.
+
+JPA transformers still own separate sessions; this change does not introduce
+state sharing between the managed and client transformer factories.
+
 
